\section*{Special Cases}
\begin{enumerate}[label={\arabic*.}, leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Convex $f$.}  When $f$ is convex, the same analysis (with a
different choice of $c_k$) yields the classical $O(1/k^{2})$ rate for the
function values.
\item \textbf{Constant momentum $\beta\in[0,1)$.}  If we fix $\beta_k\equiv\beta$,
the coefficient $\gamma_{k+1}$ becomes
$\gamma = \frac{1}{2L}\frac{1-2\beta}{1-\beta}$,
which is positive only if $\beta<\tfrac12$.  Hence a constant momentum
larger than $1/2$ may break the $O(1/T)$ guarantee in the non‑convex case.
\item \textbf{Exact stepsize $\alpha=1/L$.}  The proof crucially uses the
identity $\frac{1}{2L}-\frac{c_{k+1}}{2L^{2}} = \frac{1}{2L}$ (see
Step 14).  Choosing a larger stepsize would make the coefficient negative
and the potential function could increase, invalidating the bound.
\end{enumerate}

